    "knowledge_point_id": "ce45704e-ecea-5c67-bb40-a413f8a0d72e",
    "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
    "problem": "If $sec⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then what is $sec⁡(x+14\\pi )?$",
    "worked_solution": "The period of $y=sec⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec⁡x=sec⁡(x+2\\cdot 7\\cdot \\pi )=sec⁡(x+14\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $sec⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2},$ then $sec⁡(x+14\\pi )=\\frac{\\sqrt{11}}{2}.$",
